=== task: mcq medium
title: Why O(n)?
prompt: `longest_under` has a `while` loop inside a `for` loop. Why is it O(n), not O(n²)?
* left only moves forward, so the `while` loop runs at most n times in total, across the whole search :: Right. Together the two edges move at most 2n times.
- The `while` loop never runs :: It runs whenever the total goes over the limit.
- The list is sorted :: It doesn't need to be, and isn't, in the examples.
- Each window holds at most k items :: There's no fixed k in a variable window.
hint: How many times can left move during the whole search?
hint: Does left ever move back?
right: Each turn of the `while` loop moves left forward one place. left starts at 0 and can't pass n, so all the `while` turns together number at most n.
wrong: Count the moves of each edge over the whole search: right moves n times and left at most n times, never backwards. That's at most 2n moves: O(n).

