=== task: multi hard
title: When sliding works
prompt: Choose **every** job that a sliding window does correctly.
* Keeping the total of a window as items enter and leave :: Right. Add what enters, subtract what leaves.
* Counting the vowels in every window of a string :: Right. Add 1 for a vowel entering, take 1 away for one leaving.
* Finding the longest run with a total at most a limit, when no number is negative :: Right. Shrinking then never makes the total bigger.
- Finding the largest item in every window by subtracting the item that leaves :: You can't subtract a maximum: when the largest item leaves, nothing tells you which item is now the largest.
- Finding the longest run with a total at most a limit, when numbers can be negative :: Dropping a negative number makes the total bigger, so the window can throw away items it needed.
- Finding two numbers anywhere in the list that add up to a target :: The two numbers needn't be next to each other, so they don't form a window.
hint: Sliding needs a value you can update as items enter and leave.
hint: A variable window also needs shrinking to make the window "better".
right: Totals and counts update with one addition and one subtraction, and non-negative numbers make shrinking safe. Maximums can't be "subtracted", negatives break shrinking, and a pair anywhere isn't a window.
wrong: Sliding works when the window's value can be updated item by item (sums, counts), and for variable windows when shrinking moves the total the right way. A maximum, negative numbers or items that aren't neighbours break it.

